import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.B8TleWq7.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Architecture","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/architecture.md","filePath":"guides/architecture.md"}'),n={name:"guides/architecture.md"};function s(d,e,i,l,c,h){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="architecture" tabindex="-1">Architecture <a class="header-anchor" href="#architecture" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Architecture&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page records what Keel decided and why. Each system has its own guide with the details.</p><h2 id="foundations" tabindex="-1">Foundations <a class="header-anchor" href="#foundations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Foundations&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>One shared framework</strong>, copied unchanged into every game. Game code is built on top of it and never edits it.</td><td>A bug is fixed once, and a new game starts with loading, saving and networking already working.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Keel&#39;s master copy is its own repository.</strong> Each game commits a full copy; <code>keel.json</code> records the version and <code>tools/keel.js</code> installs and updates it from a tag.</td><td>Each game is self-contained and stays on a known version. Upgrading is a deliberate step.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Keel is written in-house</strong>, with only four external libraries copied in (Trove, Packet, ProfileStore, Cmdr), each at one recorded version.</td><td>Full control of the core. Libraries are used only where they replace hard, proven work.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The server is the authority</strong> for currency, inventory, progress, damage, health, cooldowns, purchases and round state. The client is trusted only for its own movement, camera, UI and cosmetic effects.</td><td>Clients can be exploited. The client sends what it wants to do; the server decides.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Keel is <code>--!strict</code>.</strong> Game code is strict by default and may opt out per file.</td><td>Typos and wrong-shaped data are caught before pressing Play.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="layout" tabindex="-1">Layout <a class="header-anchor" href="#layout" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Layout&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Script Sync can only sync Folders, so there are six root Folders (see <a href="/Keel-Docs/guides/boot-and-folders">Folders and boot</a>). Server code is never sent to clients: <code>KeelServer</code> and <code>Server</code> live in ServerScriptService. Client code lives in <code>ReplicatedFirst</code>, which is not cloned into players the way <code>StarterPlayerScripts</code> is.</p><h2 id="how-code-runs" tabindex="-1">How code runs <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-code-runs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How code runs&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>One entry per side.</strong> <code>Server/Main.server.luau</code> and the <code>Boot</code> LocalScript. A single place controls start order, so scripts never race.</li><li><strong>Modules declare their dependencies</strong> and the <a href="/Keel-Docs/guides/loader-and-scheduler">Loader</a> sorts them. Folder order never matters, and a cycle stops boot with an error.</li><li><strong>Lifecycle:</strong> <code>Init</code> wires things up and must not wait or call other modules. <code>Start</code> runs after every <code>Init</code> and may wait. <code>Update</code> is optional and driven by one central Scheduler.</li><li><strong>Every call is guarded.</strong> Init, Start and Update are wrapped in <code>pcall</code> and timed, so one broken module logs an error instead of stopping everything.</li><li><strong>Services and Controllers.</strong> Server systems are Services; client systems are Controllers.</li><li><strong>Dependencies point one way.</strong> Server: Services, then Components, then Shared. Client: UI, then Controllers, then Shared.</li><li><strong>Lower layers report upward through typed Signals</strong> (<code>Health.Died:Connect(...)</code>). There is no global event bus keyed by name strings, because those hide who listens and fail silently on typos.</li></ul><h2 id="state-replicates-messages-carry-intent" tabindex="-1">State replicates; messages carry intent <a class="header-anchor" href="#state-replicates-messages-carry-intent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;State replicates; messages carry intent&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Ongoing values (cash, health, inventory) reach clients as <strong>state they watch</strong>. Network messages are only for requests (&quot;buy this&quot;) and one-off moments (a hit landed). Each piece of player state declares its audience:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Audience</th><th>Receives it</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Owner</code></td><td>only that player</td><td>cash, inventory, quests, cooldowns</td></tr><tr><td><code>Public</code></td><td>everyone</td><td>level, team, equipped item</td></tr><tr><td><code>None</code></td><td>the server only</td><td>anti-cheat counters</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Data about a world object (an enemy&#39;s health, a door) goes in attributes on its model, so with streaming on it arrives and leaves with the model. See <a href="/Keel-Docs/guides/state-and-replica">State and Replica</a>.</p><h2 id="networking" tabindex="-1">Networking <a class="header-anchor" href="#networking" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Networking&quot;">​</a></h2><p>One central list in <code>Shared/Net</code> holds every message, named <code>Feature.Action</code>. Each definition declares argument types, ranges and rate limits, and Net checks them <strong>before</strong> any handler runs. A client may send only messages that declare a <code>Rate</code>. See <a href="/Keel-Docs/guides/networking">Networking</a>.</p><h2 id="player-data" tabindex="-1">Player data <a class="header-anchor" href="#player-data" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Player data&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Saving wraps ProfileStore: a session lock (one server owns a profile), load retries, and a shutdown that waits for saves. Keel adds kicking on load failure, a 60-second autosave, versioned migrations, a store name per environment (Studio, Test, Live) and purchase de-duplication. Components declare their own saved shape. See <a href="/Keel-Docs/guides/player-data">Player data</a>.</p><h2 id="game-data-defs" tabindex="-1">Game data (Defs) <a class="header-anchor" href="#game-data-defs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Game data (Defs)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Defs are static, authored data about what things <em>are</em>, separate from player data (what each player <em>has</em>). One typed module per kind, permanent string ids, and boot-time validation of every entry and cross-reference. In Studio a broken entry stops boot; on a live server only that entry is disabled. See <a href="/Keel-Docs/guides/defs">Defs</a>.</p><h2 id="client-and-ui" tabindex="-1">Client and UI <a class="header-anchor" href="#client-and-ui" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Client and UI&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Controllers are the only client modules that send network messages. Input is bound by action, not by key. UI is hand-built: Views (one per screen), Widgets (reusable pieces) and a UIController that opens and closes them. See <a href="/Keel-Docs/guides/client-ui">Client UI</a>.</p><h2 id="external-libraries" tabindex="-1">External libraries <a class="header-anchor" href="#external-libraries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;External libraries&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each is an unchanged copy of one pinned version in a <code>Vendor/</code> folder, reached only through a re-export file next to it, so swapping a library changes one file.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Library</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trove (Sleitnick)</td><td>cleanup bag for connections, instances and threads</td></tr><tr><td>Packet (Suphi Kaner)</td><td>binary networking under Keel&#39;s Net</td></tr><tr><td>ProfileStore (loleris)</td><td>session-locked saving under Data</td></tr><tr><td>Cmdr (evaera)</td><td>the admin console</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Signal (typed events) is written in-house. Details: <a href="/Keel-Docs/guides/tooling#vendored-libraries">Tooling and hooks</a>.</p>',24)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
